Z||000000||Philphine||07-20-2001||09:34 PM||||i've mentioned a few times before the '86 gt i found at a pawn shop. hopefully in a few weeks i'll have it at home. they told me it was running when they took it in but a mechanic they had look at it said it now needed a fuel pump. i've done that a few times so if thats what it is i'm not too worried. i am wondering if there is something i can do to the pump that is in the tank now without removing it.<P>like could i maybe pour carb cleaner or something similar down the fuel line to disolve any possible gumming up? or even compressed air or a combo of the two?<P>i know it's possible that with it having sat so long (they said they took it in in '99) there could be other problems like the tank rusting or clogged injectors (among other things not associated with the fuel system), but if there are easier things to try before i drop the tank i like to try them first. thanks for any help.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-21-2001||01:40 AM||||I don't know how useful this would be, but you could add an electric fuel pump between the fuel tank and the filter. That way, the only thing the in-tank pump would have to do is create enough pressure to supply the add on pump.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||07-21-2001||10:22 AM||||Why do auto maker put fuel pumps inside the tank to begin with? ||||reg||5
Z||000003||Mach10||07-21-2001||01:09 PM||||1) Cooling<BR>2) Harder to Airlock<BR>3) More efficient<BR>4) Self Priming<BR>5) Harder for people to work on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
